<p class="eyebrow">Python · AI Search · Competitive intelligence</p><h1>AI Search Competitive Intelligence</h1>
<p>An independent benchmark comparing Notion, Asana, ClickUp and monday.com across the same controlled buyer-intent prompt set. This is research, not client work for any benchmarked company.</p>
<section class="project-summary" aria-label="Project at a glance"><p class="eyebrow">At a glance / Evidence status</p><div class="summary-grid"><div><h2>Problem</h2><p>How can four SaaS platforms be compared in AI-generated answers without confusing brand mentions with citations or recommendations?</p></div><div><h2>Method & tools</h2><p>35 versioned buyer-intent prompts, seven intent families, public-source evidence, structured observation fields and Python-oriented scoring rules.</p></div><div><h2>Deliverables</h2><p>Benchmark protocol, measurement definitions and an auditable observation design.</p></div><div><h2>Verified status</h2><p>Protocol prepared; AI response observations and comparative performance results have not yet been collected or published.</p></div></div><p class="summary-link"><a href="/ai-search/">Read the measurement methodology →</a></p></section>

<h2>Dataset v1</h2><p><strong>35 frozen prompts</strong> across seven intent families: category discovery, alternatives, comparison, capability, pricing/value, implementation and recommendation. Prompt definitions are versioned before observations are scored.</p>
<h2>Measurement model</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Mention coverage</h3><p>Share of eligible prompts in which each brand is explicitly mentioned.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Citation coverage</h3><p>Share of eligible prompts with evidence cited for the brand.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Recommendation coverage</h3><p>Share of recommendation prompts in which a brand is included.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Intent consistency</h3><p>Family-level visibility is preserved so a strong result in one intent cannot hide weakness in another.</p></article></div>
<h2>Audit trail</h2><p>Every observation records engine/run, prompt, brand, mention, citation, recommendation inclusion, ordered position where meaningful, source type, source URL and an evidence note. Repeated runs append evidence instead of overwriting history.</p>
<h2>Scoring guardrail</h2><p>Mean position is calculated only for explicitly ordered shortlists and is never presented as a universal AI-search rank. Missing observations are never imputed. The Python pipeline refuses to invent results.</p>
<h2>What the code actually verifies</h2>
<p>The benchmark now includes a working Python validation and scoring pipeline, with <strong>15 locally passing unit tests</strong>. It rejects invalid observation values, duplicate run cells, mismatched prompt families and citations without a source URL. Comparisons use only complete four-brand rounds sharing the same date, engine and prompt: incomplete rounds are counted but not misclassified as brand absence. Unmeasured metrics remain unmeasured.</p>
<p>These tests demonstrate measurement and data-quality engineering. <strong>They do not demonstrate that any of the four products currently ranks, receives citations or is recommended by an AI system.</strong></p>
